# Stationery

Stationery writes PDFs from Ruby classes. A document is a class, `view_template` describes the
page with elements (text, rows, boxes, tables, images, barcodes), and the engine measures, places
and paginates them: there is no cursor to move and no coordinate to work out. It is pure Ruby with
no runtime dependency; Inter ships inside the gem, so a document needs no font to start.

This skill was written for stationery <%= version %>. `stationery --version` says which version the
application has; `stationery skill status` says whether this skill is current for it.

## The model in a page

```ruby
class InvoicePdf < Stationery::Document
  page size: :a4, margin: 40                         # class level: once for every render
  default_text size: 9, color: "#1F2937"
  metadata title: "Invoice", lang: "en"
  strict                                             # raise Stationery::WarningsError instead of writing a file that warned
  footer { |page| text "Page #{page.number} of #{page.count}", size: 7, align: :right }   # every page

  def self.preview = new(Invoice.sample)             # what `stationery render` renders

  def initialize(invoice)
    super()                                          # always: the builder needs it
    @invoice = invoice
  end

  def view_template                                  # instance level: what is on the pages
    row(align: :middle) do
      column(width: 0.6) { text "Invoice #{@invoice.number}", size: 20, weight: :bold }
      column { image "app/assets/images/logo.png", height: 32, align: :right }
    end
    spacer 16
    table(@invoice.rows, width: :full, header: true) { |t| t.columns(1..).align = :right }
  end
end

pdf = InvoicePdf.new(invoice).to_pdf                 # a binary String; to_pdf("a.pdf") also writes it
```

- The body is a flow: children stack top to bottom and the page breaks where the next child does
  not fit. `row { column { } }` puts things side by side, `box` pads, paints and borders, `table`
  is for tabular data, `columns(count: 2)` pours text newspaper style, `float: :left` wraps text
  around an image or a box.
- Class-level declarations (`page`, `font_family`, `default_text`, `metadata`, `header`, `footer`,
  `page_template`, `conformance`, `tagged`, `print`, `monochrome`, `strict`) apply to every
  render; most have a `to_pdf(...)` option of the same name for one render.
- Header, footer and page template blocks are written at class level but run on the document
  instance, with `page.number` and `page.count` known: instance variables and private methods
  work in them. Without `on:` a header or footer is on every page, a cover included. With a
  cover, write `footer(on: :rest) { … }`: the pages `on:` does not match have no footer, so no
  empty `footer(on: :first) {}` is needed (it is for taking a footer away from pages an earlier
  declaration gave one). `on:` also takes `:odd`, `:even`, `:last`, a number, a Range or a lambda.
- A file handed to `stationery render` needs no `require "stationery"`: the command has loaded the
  gem. A script run with `ruby` does, and a Rails app loads it from the Gemfile.
- Reusable pieces are `Stationery::Component` classes, drawn with `render MyComponent.new(...)`.
- In Rails: `gem "stationery", require: "stationery/rails"`, then `render pdf: InvoicePdf.new(@invoice)`
  or `send_pdf(...)` in a controller; documents usually live in `app/pdfs/`.

## Always look at what you rendered

A PDF that renders without an error can still be wrong: a column too narrow, text over a picture,
a table cut in the wrong place, a blank second page. Never report a document as done without
looking at it. After every change:

1. Render it and write a picture of each page:

   ```sh
   stationery render app/pdfs/invoice_pdf.rb --png          # invoice_pdf.pdf and invoice_pdf-1.png, …
   stationery render app/pdfs/invoice_pdf.rb --png-only --pages 1 --dpi 144   # a closer look
   ```

   `render` loads the file and renders the document it defines, from `def self.preview` when
   `initialize` takes arguments. In a Rails app, where the file needs the app loaded, write the
   pictures from a runner: `bin/rails runner 'InvoicePdf.new(Invoice.last).to_png("tmp/invoice.png")'`
   (one page writes `tmp/invoice.png`, several `tmp/invoice-1.png`, `tmp/invoice-2.png`, …).
2. **Open every PNG and look at it** (read the image file). Check what a person would: nothing cut
   off, overlapping or running off the page, columns lined up, totals where they belong, no page
   that is empty or holds one stray line. The pictures do not draw form fields (they are the PDF
   viewer's); for a form, `mutool draw -r 96 -o form-%d.png form.pdf` draws them when MuPDF is
   installed.
3. Read the warnings. `stationery render` prints them to stderr; in code, `doc.warnings` after
   any render (`to_pdf`, `to_png`, `to_zpl`) lists overflows, missing glyphs, skipped images,
   unresolved links and the rest, each with a `message`. Fix every one: a warning is a document
   that is not what was written.
4. **Count the pages.** Content that does not fit moves to a next page without a warning, and
   `strict` does not catch it: a page that should be one (a label, a receipt, a one-page invoice)
   is checked by its page count (`--png` writes `-1.png`, `-2.png`; `have_page_count(1)` in a spec;
   `doc.to_zpl.scan("^XA").size` for labels).
5. **Decode every barcode** from the picture: `zbarimg -q invoice_pdf-1.png` (`brew install zbar`)
   prints each code it reads with its data; a code it cannot read will not scan at the till either.
6. When you cannot look at a picture, or to compare two renders, print the page as text:
   `stationery inspect app/pdfs/invoice_pdf.pdf` (or the `.rb` file, rendered first) lists each
   page's text lines with their position, font and size, its images, links and form fields in
   points from the top-left corner. It needs the `pdf-reader` gem.
7. When a layout is puzzling, `to_pdf(debug: true)` (`stationery render FILE --debug`) outlines
   every box, column, cell and image in colour.

Then make the checks stay: `strict` on a line of its own in the class body (or
`to_pdf(strict: true)`, `to_zpl(strict: true)` for one render) raises `Stationery::WarningsError`,
whose `warnings` are the render's, instead of writing a file with warnings; `stationery render
--strict` exits 1. A spec holds the text, pages and conformance
(see [reference/testing.md](reference/testing.md)):

```ruby
require "stationery/rspec"                                # gem "pdf-reader" in the test group

RSpec.describe InvoicePdf do
  subject(:pdf) { InvoicePdf.new(invoice) }

  it { is_expected.to have_pdf_text("Invoice INV-7") }
  it { is_expected.to have_page_count(1) }
  it { is_expected.to have_no_warnings }
end
```

## Rules that surprise

- **A String is literal.** `text "<b>Total</b>"` prints the tags. `text "<b>Total</b>", markup: true`
  reads `<b> <i> <u> <br> <color rgb=""> <font size=""> <link href="">` and HTML entities, so
  escape user data first (`ERB::Util.html_escape(name)`) when you wrap it in tags. Styled runs
  in Ruby need no escaping: `text { b "Total"; plain " due" }`. `html` and `markdown` are for
  rich text from users or a CMS.
- **A Float up to 1 is a share of the width; anything else is points.** `column(width: 0.5)`,
  `box(width: 0.4)` and `image(width: 1.0)` are shares. `width: 1` (an Integer) is one point,
  `width: 1/3r` (a Rational) a third of a point: write `0.333`. `table(width:)`, `svg` and `barcode`
  take points only: `table(..., width: 0.5)` is a table half a point wide, and
  `table(..., width: :full)` the whole width. `width: :auto` is the content's natural width, `nil`
  an equal share of what is left. An `svg` without `width:` is drawn at its viewBox size in points,
  shrunk to fit. **A width that is too small does not warn**: the text is set a letter per line,
  and only the picture shows it.
- **Lengths are points** (72 to an inch). `mm(20)`, `cm(2)`, `inch(4)` and `pt(12)` convert in any
  document or component; `page size: "4in x 6in", margin: "3mm"` reads units. Page sizes:
  `:a4`, `:a5`, `:a6`, `:letter`, `:legal`, `:label_4x6`, `:label_100x150`, `:dl`, … or
  `[w, h]`; `layout: :landscape` turns it. Coordinates (`at: [x, y]`, a canvas) are from the
  top-left corner of the page, y downwards.
- **What splits and what moves whole.** Text splits between lines, tables between rows (the
  header row repeats with `header: true`), lists between items, boxes, rows and columns with
  their content. An image, a rule, a canvas, a barcode, a rotated box, a `stack` and a float move
  to the next page whole. A box that fits on the next page moves there whole; one taller than a
  page continues across pages (`break_inside: :avoid` never splits it, `:auto` always may).
- **A fixed `height:` never splits** a box (nor a row holding it) and does not clip unless told:
  `overflow: :truncate` or `:shrink_to_fit`. Use `min_height:` for a floor that may still split.
- **Keep a heading with what follows**: `text "Totals", keep_with_next: true` (or a number of
  points); `group(keep_together: true) { }` keeps a block on one page. `spacer` at a page break
  is dropped; `page_break` starts a new page.
- **Constants resolve where the code is written, not in the document.** A method in a module
  included into a document that names `ACCENT` looks for it in the module and at the top level,
  not in the document class, and raises `NameError`. Put shared constants in the module, or write
  `self.class::ACCENT`.
- **`print` in a class body** is the print-hints declaration; inside methods it is still
  `Kernel#print`.
- **Images and fonts are read from paths** (relative to the process's working directory: use
  `Rails.root.join(...)` or `File.expand_path(..., __dir__)`) or IOs; nothing is fetched from a
  URL. JPEG, PNG, lossless WebP and SVG (`svg`) only.
- **Floats** (`float: :left` on an `image` or a `box`, with a `width:`) wrap everything that follows
  them in the same flow, not just the next paragraph, until the float ends. Put the float and its
  text in a `box { }` so what comes after starts below it. A float never splits.
- **`box(at: [x, y])` takes no room in the flow**: it is placed on the page and the content goes on
  under it. Follow it with a `spacer` of its height when the flow should start below it.
- **`columns(count: 2)`** balances the columns by default and continues across pages; there is no
  element that spans them (end the block, write the full-width part, start another).
- A character no font has draws as an empty box and warns (`MissingGlyph`); add a family with
  `font_family` and `font_fallbacks`, or install a pack (`stationery fonts install noto_sans`).

## Conformance, labels and printing, in brief

- `conformance :pdf_a3b` (archival), `:pdf_ua1` (accessible: needs `metadata title:, lang:`,
  `alt:` on every image and svg, headings in order) or both. What would break the claim raises
  `Stationery::ConformanceError` instead of writing a mislabelled file; `missing_glyphs: :replace`
  keeps a PDF/A claim when a customer's name has a character no font has.
  `factur_x(profile: :en16931) { xml }` makes a Factur-X/ZUGFeRD e-invoice. Validate with veraPDF:
  `verapdf --format text -v --flavour 3b invoice.pdf` (`brew install verapdf`; Docker works too).
  See [reference/conformance.md](reference/conformance.md).
- `barcode "SX0042", type: :code128` (or `:ean13`, `:qr`, `:datamatrix`) draws vector bars.
  `to_zpl` writes ZPL for a thermal label printer, `monochrome dpi: 203` makes the PDF print
  well on one, and `print scaling: :none` stops "fit to page" shrinking a label. See
  [reference/printing.md](reference/printing.md).

